<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nashik</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nasik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nashik is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the Godavari river north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>. The Pandavleni caves nearby, cut between the 1st century BCE and the 3rd century CE, preserve inscriptions of the Satavahanas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abhira-people">Abhiras</a> that are key sources for the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. A sacred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> city associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, it is one of the four sites of the Kumbh Mela.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nashik.jpg" alt="Nashik" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Savitr1915 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NashikViewfromPandavLeni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
